# Line endings
#
# Everything text is stored in the repository with LF and checked out with LF,
# on every machine. Without this, Git obeys the local `core.autocrlf` — which is
# `true` on the Windows box this project is developed on — so a checkout would
# rewrite every file to CRLF and the next commit from a Mac or from CI would
# show every line as changed. Editors and Node handle LF on Windows fine; the
# churn is the only thing that actually breaks.
#
# `text=auto` still lets Git decide what is text at all, so binaries below are
# belt and braces rather than the only protection.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must keep LF even if a rule above is ever relaxed — a CRLF
# shebang line makes the interpreter unfindable and the script dies with an
# error that names the wrong thing entirely.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Media pipeline output (apps/web/public/media/) — committed on purpose, and
# must never be passed through line-ending or diff machinery.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.mp4 binary
*.webm binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.pdf binary

# Generated: keep it out of diffs and out of language statistics. It is still
# committed and still reviewed when it changes — this only stops a 114 kB
# lockfile from burying an actual change in a diff.
pnpm-lock.yaml -diff linguist-generated=true
